value.fix:
The loop copies any field the client names in `requestedFields` from `user.data` into the response. A caller can ask for sensitive Personal Data Elements (email, phone, address, password hash, tokens, internal flags) and get them back. Replace it with a server-side allow-list based on what the requester is entitled to see, and intersect the requested fields with it. Example:

```ts
const PUBLIC_USER_FIELDS = ['id', 'displayName', 'avatarUrl'] as const;
const SELF_USER_FIELDS = [...PUBLIC_USER_FIELDS, 'email', 'phone'] as const; // only for the owner or an authorised admin
type AllowedField = (typeof SELF_USER_FIELDS)[number];

const allowed: readonly AllowedField[] =
  requester?.id === user?.data.id || requester?.isAdmin ? SELF_USER_FIELDS : PUBLIC_USER_FIELDS;

const safeFields = (Array.isArray(requestedFields) ? requestedFields : [])
  .filter((f): f is AllowedField => typeof f === 'string' && (allowed as readonly string[]).includes(f));

for (const field of safeFields) {
  const value = user?.data[field];
  if (value !== undefined) baseUser[field] = value;
}
```

Also: (1) never put secrets such as passwordHash, tokens, salts or internal IDs on any allow-list; (2) type `baseUser` as a defined response DTO (e.g. `Partial<Pick<UserData, AllowedField>>`), not an open record; (3) reject or ignore unknown or prototype keys like `__proto__` and `constructor`, which the `includes` check already does; (4) move this filtering into one shared `serializeUser(user, requester, requestedFields)` helper and use it on every route that returns user records, so the same field limits apply everywhere as Rule 6(1)(b) requires; (5) add tests showing that requesting a forbidden field (e.g. `?fields=email,passwordHash` as a non-owner) leaves it out of the response.
